        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65665,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> fibroepithelial polyp; cellular pseudosarcomatous fibroepithelial stromal polyp; pseudosarcoma botryoides; mesodermal stromal polyp</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65666,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65667,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroepithelial stromal polyp most commonly occurs in the vagina but can also be seen in the vulva or (rarely) cervix {10672053}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65668,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroepithelial stromal polyps are often discovered incidentally. Symptoms may include bleeding, discharge, or discomfort {10672053; 25925211; 15626915}. Cases usually present as a solitary lesion; however, multiple polyps may occur during pregnancy.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65669,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroepithelial stromal polyp frequently occurs during pregnancy but may also occur in postmenopausal women undergoing hormone replacement therapy, or rarely in infants {10672053; 25925211; 15626915; 19542922}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65670,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65671,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroepithelial stromal polyp is thought to arise from hormonally responsive subepithelial stromal cells of the lower female genital tract.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65672,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroepithelial stromal polyps are usually &lt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;cm but may be as large as 18.5&nbsp;cm {23842282}. They range from sessile to pedunculated to villiform and are soft to rubbery and greyish pink.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65673,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibroepithelial stromal polyp is composed of three components: a central fibrovascular core, a pedunculated or polypoid proliferation of stroma, and overlying squamous epithelium. The most characteristic feature is the presence of stellate and multinucleated stromal cells, which tend to aggregate along the stromal&ndash;epithelial junction and around the blood vessels of the central fibrovascular core {10672053; 25925211; 15626915}. The polyp may exhibit a significant degree of stromal cellularity, nuclear pleomorphism, increased mitotic activity of &gt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup> (equating to &gt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;mitoses/10&nbsp;HPF of 0.5&nbsp;mm in diameter and 0.2&nbsp;mm<sup>2</sup> in area), and atypical mitoses (pseudosarcomatous change of stromal cells) {10680891; 3229895}. The overlying squamous epithelium may be hyperplastic. The stromal cells are consistently positive for desmin, ER, and PR and are sometimes positive for SMA {10672053; 25925211; 15626915}. Myogenin positivity may be rarely seen {23944986}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65674,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 418,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-12T17:21:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
